Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Declare the runtime tools Nonoka X needs, and the ffmpeg that runs them

Reason: Nonoka X has no resource system of its own -- every managed binary it can
find lives under `finesub/runtime/<id>/`, resolved by `managedtools` -- so a tool
has to be declared here to be installable at all. Two separate corrections to the
packaged manifest, both about which binary lands on the user's machine.

**The ffmpeg variant.** The manifest pins BtbN's **lgpl** build, which is
configured `--disable-libx264 --disable-libx265`, while every clip the engine
encodes asks for `libx264` -- `VIDEO_ENCODER_ARGS` (the video window clips,
Gemini REST path included), `transcode_video_for_agy`, and
`containerize_audio_for_agy`, which wraps an audio window in an MP4 with one
black frame because agy rejects a bare audio MIME type. So the moment a run uses
anything but text as its correction reference, ffmpeg answers:

  [vost#0:0] Unknown encoder 'libx264'
  Error opening output files: Encoder not found

Switching the variant rather than the encoder keeps every clip byte-identical to
what the engine was written and calibrated for. libopenh264 is present in the
lgpl build but takes a bitrate rather than `-crf`, has no `-tune stillimage`, and
would silently change what the model sees; a runtime encoder probe would do the
same, differently on each machine, against the reproducibility `describe_ffmpeg`
exists for. The GPL variant comes from the same release under the same
digest-from-the-release-API rule, onto the user's own machine at provisioning
time -- Nonoka X redistributes no ffmpeg binary, and invoking one as a subprocess
is not derivation. Changing `required_files` is also what re-provisions an
existing lgpl install: `status()` grades a version dir by those paths, so the old
directory reads as missing and is replaced.

**The video downloader's optional tools.** All three are optional: without them
the downloader still runs, it is just slower and fails on anything but short
videos.

  aria2c         Multi-connection fetch. Pure speed; the built-in downloader
                 works without it.
  node           A JavaScript runtime. yt-dlp needs one to solve the "n"
                 challenge (it enables only deno by default), and the PO token
                 generator runs on it.
  pot-provider   Proof-of-origin tokens. YouTube only returns usable formats to
                 a signed-in session whose request carries one, and yt-dlp
                 cannot mint them itself.

They are declared together because they only work together: YouTube gates in
sequence, and a missing piece fails at an earlier gate than the one it belongs
to, which is what makes each look useless when added alone.

`pot-provider` carries both halves of the provider -- the yt-dlp plugin and the
generator it calls. Upstream ships no built backend (it needs an npm install
with a native `canvas`, which is not a single archive), and versions the two
halves together, so Nonoka X builds and publishes one bundle instead: see
`scripts/build_pot_bundle.py`. It is GPL-3.0, and the bundle carries the licence
and corresponding source accordingly.

Every asset is pinned by size and sha256. aria2c's binary and node's sit one
directory down inside their archives, so `required_files` names those paths.

Removal: Delete the ffmpeg half once upstream ships an encoder the lgpl build can
run or pins the GPL variant itself; delete the rest once upstream declares these
three tools in its own manifest.

